{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792831138,"nonce":"vOxRVjSnEyZoldyTZosjaCwmXEE=","title":"Python: sparse matmult loses 16% once list headers stay hoisted across its loops","message":"With list element storage as its own alias root (LICM: Buffer disjoint from objects and headers), scimark_sparsematmult goes from 117 to 136 ms on the NUC (11 to 15 interleaved reps, quartiles apart; same with ZYNTAX_DISABLE_TYPED_ALIAS=1, so it is the buffer rule). The region's HIR is otherwise unchanged; what differs is that the header loads of col and x (element pointer, length) now leave the r loop for the region entry instead of staying in it, so they are live across every level of the nest. The y[r] store keeps LLVM from rematerializing them, and the inner loop is register-bound on x86. LU gains 29% from the same change.\n\nWorth trying: hoist a load only out of the loops whose body it lies in, not past an outer loop's own store-free levels, or let the LLVM tier see the hoisted values as invariant loads it may reload.","files":null}]}